@echo off
rem Measure how long Spitfire Symphony Orchestra takes to load in MuseScore, and what could make it faster.
rem
rem 1. Close MuseScore (it would hold the orchestra's samples in memory too, and the times would be off).
rem 2. Drag the score (or scores) you want faster onto this file. Double-clicked with no score, it
rem    measures a few patches of each family (Violins 1, Violas, Celli, Violins 1 - Performance,
rem    Flutes a2, Horns a2, Trumpet Solo, Timpani).
rem
rem It starts a MuseScore of its own with no window (MuseScore --measure-load-times, see
rem mscore/soundlibraryloadtimes.h): each patch alone, the score as MuseScore loads it, the same with
rem 2 and 4 threads, an instance reused, then a probe of one patch's saved settings (which ones unload
rem samples). It uses a copy of your setups (Documents\MuseScore Sound Library Check\background load
rem times setups) and writes nothing in MuseScore's own folders. About 20 to 60 minutes. If Kontakt
rem shows a message, it is closed after 30 s and written in the report.
rem
rem When it is done, the folder Documents\MuseScore Sound Library Check opens: hand back the newest
rem "Spitfire Symphony Orchestra load times <date>.txt" (one text file; also written as it goes, so
rem send it even if the run stopped half way).
rem To stop it: Task Manager, end @MSCORE_OUTPUT_NAME@.exe (the one without a window).

start "" "%~dp0@MSCORE_OUTPUT_NAME@.exe" --measure-load-times "Spitfire Symphony Orchestra" %*

echo The load times are being measured in the background (scores: %*).
echo Progress: %USERPROFILE%\Documents\MuseScore Sound Library Check\background load times.log
echo That folder opens when it is done. Keep MuseScore closed until then.
timeout /t 15
